  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a mobile phone mast?",
      "a": "A mast, or base station, holds antennas that send and receive radio signals from phones in its cell. It is linked by fibre or microwave radio to the operator's network, which connects calls and data onwards.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a cell tower?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is handover?",
      "a": "Handover is when your call or data session is switched from one mast to another as you move, without cutting off. The network prepares the new mast first, then tells your phone to jump across.",
      "asks": [
        "How does my call keep going when I travel?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What decides when to hand over?",
      "a": "Your phone measures the signal from nearby masts and reports back. The network compares them, and when a neighbour is clearly stronger, or the current mast is too busy, it orders the handover.",
      "asks": [
        "Who decides which mast my phone uses?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do calls drop?",
      "a": "A call can drop if your phone moves somewhere no mast covers well, like a tunnel, or if the new mast is too busy to take it, or if the signal fades too fast for handover to finish.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does my call cut out on the train?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are cells smaller in cities?",
      "a": "Each mast has a limited amount of radio capacity to share. In crowded cities there are many more phones, so operators use lots of small cells close together, each serving fewer people.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are there so many masts in towns?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Mobile networks are called cellular because the area they cover is divided into cells, each served by a base station.",
    "In 4G networks a handover usually interrupts data for only a few tens of milliseconds."
  ],
